  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "Double strokes let the stick do half the work: one motion, two notes. They are the engine of rolls, of fast fills that sound relaxed, and of every rudiment with a diddle in it. The challenge is the second stroke: it wants to be weaker and late.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Open (slow): two separate wrist strokes, each from the same height, each with full rebound. Closed (fast): one wrist motion throws the stick, the first note rebounds, and the fingers pull the stick back into the pad for the second note, which comes out as loud as the first. In between there is a zone where the wrist and fingers share the work; take your time there.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "Line 3 accents the second stroke on purpose: that is the note to train. Never let the doubles turn into a buzz, you should always hear two clear notes. Lines 4 and 5 change gear between eighth and sixteenth doubles: the hand motion stays the same size, only the timing changes. Line 7 starts every double with the left hand.",
  ),
